\paragraph{Stage 3: Sustained Collapse (Gen~3$\to$5).}
Both spectral indicators reverse direction at Generation~3 and decline monotonically through Generation~5.  The effective rank drops from its peak of 496.44 to 493.31, 485.67, and 479.21 at Generations~3, 4, and 5 respectively, a cumulative decline of $-3.5\%$ from peak.  The spectral log-volume falls from 2469.93 to 2440.57, 2405.05, and 2370.17, a steeper cumulative decline of $-4.0\%$.  The per-generation rate of effective rank decline accelerates: $-0.6\%$ (Gen~2$\to$3), $-1.5\%$ (Gen~3$\to$4), and $-1.3\%$ (Gen~4$\to$5).  This acceleration confirms that spectral collapse is a self-reinforcing process rather than a transient perturbation.

The asymmetry between spectral log-volume and effective rank declines persists throughout the collapse phase.  The spectral log-volume decreases at approximately 1.15$\times$ the rate of effective rank because it accumulates contributions from the full tail of the singular spectrum, whereas effective rank (spectral entropy) is dominated by the top singular values.  This differential sensitivity supports the use of spectral log-volume as the primary early-warning signal in our closed-loop controller (\S3.3).  Preliminary results from the 1B pilot suggest that the spectral log-volume may also lead temporally: at Generation~2, the spectral log-volume has already begun declining while the effective rank remains at its peak.

\paragraph{Leading Singular Value and Spectral Concentration.}
The leading singular value $\sigma_1$ exhibits a non-monotone trajectory that illuminates the collapse mechanism.  During decompression (Gen~0$\to$3), $\sigma_1$ declines continuously from 1942.8 to 700.7 as variance disperses across dimensions.  At Generation~4, this trend reverses: $\sigma_1$ rises to 729.6 and continues to 741.0 at Generation~5.  The reversal of $\sigma_1$ coincides precisely with the acceleration of effective rank decline, confirming that collapse operates through spectral concentration.  Variance migrates from tail dimensions back into the leading principal component, producing a representation space that is increasingly dominated by a single direction.  The simultaneous decrease of effective rank and increase of $\sigma_1$ after Generation~3 provides geometric evidence that recursive self-training induces a winner-take-all dynamic in the singular spectrum.

\subsection{Collapse Indicators Cross-Validation}
\label{sec:cross_validation}

Table~\ref{tab:phase0_metrics} summarizes all tracked metrics for Generations~0--5.

